  "insights": [
   "AI is expected to meaningfully boost productivity growth over the next decade, though historical lessons from the ICT revolution suggest the path to broad-based gains is neither fast nor uniform.",
   "The ICT productivity boom arrived 15 years after the commercialization of the personal computer, with a J-curve effect where productivity growth initially dragged before accelerating.",
   "Three factors delayed ICT productivity gains: high initial component costs, the time required for network effects to reach critical mass, and the necessity of complementary intangible investment in organizational restructuring.",
   "AI productivity impacts may be quicker than ICT due to faster cost declines for models and less reliance on network effects, but human and institutional adoption obstacles remain significant.",
   "Industries with the highest potential for early AI-driven productivity gains include information, professional services, insurance, and finance, based on composite rankings of AI exposure and adoption.",
